Dear Mark,

  Let me begin by saying that the most important goal I have is to preserve
our fruitful and pleasant professional relationship.  I have enjoyed working
with you and have benefitted from your knowledge, perspective, and willingness
to be helpful.

  In pursuit of that goal, my first thought is that I should say OK I accept
your strongly felt desire to submit the pie experiment paper without my name
on it.  However, my second thought is that I would feel uncomfortable with
that decision and let me try to explain my feelings so that you can understand
where I come from better - thereby leading to a continuing good sense of
cooperation between us.

  The Pie menu idea is clever and probably useful - I think it will be
applied.  I understood that it was Don's invention, nurtured by his
relationship with you.  You should publish a long basic paper on the idea and
its implementation.

  Jack participated in the 828E seminar on User Interface Design with only
10-12 students.  Jack came with little knowledge about experimental methods
and worked very hard to construct, administer and write-up a nicely done
evaluation of the Pie-menu idea.  I consider that I was directly involved
approximately twice a week in guiding the development of the experiment.
The strategy for developing experiments in a 15-week course is something
that I have been subtly refining for 11 years - it includes a sequence of
checkpoints from first proposal, to submission of materials, to pilot testing,
to administration methods, statistical analysis and reporting.  My outline
for writeups helps decompose a complex process into a series of manageable
steps.  If this framework was so smooth that it was barely visible, then I
have succeeded BUT unfortunately the perception that I hear from you is that
I was only a tangential participant in Jack's work.  It does not seem likely
to me that Jack would have conducted the experiment during the 15 weeks
of the fall semester without his involvement with me and the course.

  Over the years, one of my great prides is that so many student projects have
gone on to become published papers - undergrad as well as grad students.
I am pleased to bring students to the frontier of research in a short time -
they benefit from the learning experience and the field benefits from the good
work that is done.

  Now for the credit issue.  I have had students who did a paper for
course and then have gone on to publish on their own.  However, a term-length
experimental project that is half the course grade and which receives
regular feedback from me in discussions and on written materials is somewhat
different.  I therefore feel it appropriate to have may name listed on the
pie-experiment paper.  

  Maybe a face-to-face meeting is necessary to sort out the emotional reactions
concerning this issue (this is the conventional wisdom), but maybe we are
doing well to using this electronic forum to make more thoughtful comments.
Please let me know what you think and feel.

  Looking forward to further cooperation.


-- Ben


