Subject: Re: pie menus

I find your ideas about Pie Menus very interesting.  Your tests of the
system with new users is a refreshing taste of true Human Factors
research.  

However,  in some of the work I do,  we use menus with a relatively
large number of possible choices at one level.  Although
I have not seen Pie Menus in action,  it seems that they may be
limited to relatively few primary choices.  Assuming the text display
of each choice must be entirely contained by its slice and
the text is displayed upright (as opposed to rotated),  as
the number of choices goes up the amount of space to display all
choices appears to go up exponentially (it could be a small power).
I imagine that these menus work well for menus with up to six
choices.  However,  for menus with ten to twelve choices,  the
menu may be to large to be useful.

General purpose menu systems seem to have come to a consensus that
two-levels are required to represent alternatives in most of today's
applications (MacIntosh, XMenu, etc).  I can think of a couple of ways
Pie Menus can be extended to support two-dimensional choices.
Are you considering any particular method?

				David Harrison
				(davidh@ic.Berkeley.EDU)
				Electronics Research Laboratory
				UC Berkeley





