Depending on what version you have, there should be a conditional at
the end of piemenu.ps that checks to see if the key
/DontSetDefaultMenu is defined in systemdict. So if you go:

systemdict /DontSetDefaultMenu true put
(NeWS/piemenu.ps) LoadFile pop
systemdict /DontSetDefaultMenu undef

It will define the class PieMenu, but won't change DefaultMenu or flip
the style of your rootmenu. This would be the thing to do if you
wanted to load in quickwin.ps afterwards and get pie menus on your
window frames, but not everywhere else. 

The /flipstyle message is used in demomenu.ps to change between
LitePullRightMenu and SunViewMenu, and can be used the same way to
convert a tree of menus to or from pie menus. To use it, you set
DefaultMenu in systemdict to the class you want, and make a new menu
tree with /flipstyle:

/DefaultMenu LitePullRightMenu store
/rootmenu /flipstyle rootmenu send store

That will make a new copy of the rootmenu tree using the DefaultMenu
class. I changed the lookandfeelmenu so it switched between NeWS,
SunView1, and PieMenu, by setting DefaultMenu appropriatly and doing
the flipstyle thing. (the /flipsunview function is useless if you want
more that two menu styles) One thing to beware of is that after doing
a flipstyle, the variables in systemdict that refer to the rootmenu
submenus like lookandfeelmenu, rootimagemenu, rootdemomenu, etc, still
refer to the *old* menus, so sending them messages to change their
keys will not effect the new submenus of the root menu. You can 
send /searchkey messages to the new rootmenu to get references to the
actual new submenus. The thing to do is to customize your menu tree in
user.ps *before* you ever flip the menu style. (Nobody ever said
/flipstyle wasn't a hack.)

	-Don


