Subject: pies: more gesture, less menu

Ideally the pie user should think in terms of gestures, not menu
selections.  It's something you do with your hand, not with your
cursor. 

All that counts is the direction between the beginning and the end of
the gesture: the path you travel is not important, just the ultimate
displacement. This means pie gesture recognition is trivial.  You can
implement pie tracking using "atan2", or even more efficiently without
even using division (by doing some simple trig at layout time).

You can correct a mistaken gesture in progress by moving in the
appropriate direction, and of course the further the distance, the
higher the accuracy or "leverage".  You can stop in the middle of a
gesture (before releasing the mouse button or whatever), to pop up a
circular menu with labels in each possible direction.

But by providing useful feedback during the gesture, without popping
up the menu, you can emphesize the gesture and de-emphesize the menu.
You could reduce the need to ever pop up the menu, by making a pie
control area that you press down on to initiate the gesture, with
delineations or icons in appropriate directions.  Or you could have a
circular equivalant of the lisp machine status line (a static window
on the screen that tells you what actions are bound to the mouse
buttons at any particular location, time, shift key state, etc), that
told you what gestures you could make and what they would do.  What
other techniques can you think of, that would encourage gestures, and
wouldn't encourage people to think of (and operate) pies the same
inefficient way as old fashioned linear menus?

You can chunk gestures together (i.e. nested pie menus, but that's the
terminology I'm trying to get away from), but some sequences are much
easier to do than others. I think back&forth gestures are the easiest
because they leave your hand in the same position. Two gestures in the
same direction are easy because you can just start moving in that
direction while clicking the button twice. Making a 45 or 90 degree
turn is a bit harder, but I have actually taken advantage of that!  My
window manager menu has a "Zap" selection, that pops up a submenu with
the "Yes" selection in a totally different direction, because I found
that it was too easy to accidentally zap a window otherwise! Zapping
windows is like drawing a check mark at a certain angle. I've caught
myself waving my hand in that same gesture, at people who I want to
make go away!

	-Don


