The menus in your experiment with 12 items were found to be very fast,
even faster than 11 items. The menu items were numbers and the 12 item
menu was laid out like a clock. How much do you think that affected the
results -- would the error rate and speed by higher if the menu items
were words or colors or an ordering of numbers that does not correspond 
to a clock? I was not surprized (to make a gross understatement) at the
results for 8 item menus, because they are very fast 
due to the articulatory ease of the primary vertical and horizontal
and secondary diagonal gestures, and the cognative comfort of strong 
symmetries and geographical grounding. (excuse my alliterations,
it's harvest season in these parts).
Twelve item menu exploit the clock metaphor very well, but there are more
metaphors that have wider applications which can be exploited by
eight item menus, and eight is few enough items that you have a chance
of keeping them in your head at once. 
In other words, I think there are stronger reasons to recommend eight item 
menus than twelve item menus. Other interesting reasons one might want 
to use eight item menus is that it maps well to a keypad or touch tone
interface. It would be nice to traverse the same menu tree (or navagate
the same virtual cities and buildings) with the mouse, keyboard, or phone. 
The scrolling eight item pie menu fits into this spatial navagation
metaphor as a spiral staircase with landings or doors in the compass
directions, that you wind between by walking up and down the staircase. 
City streets have intersections with streets to the north, south, east, and
west, with buildings on the corners to the ne, nw, sw, and se. 
Inside the buildings there are halls and doors and rooms.

